  "worked_solution": "The position of the particle at $t=\\pi $ is equal to its position at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\pi ].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(\\pi )=x(\\frac{\\pi }{3})+{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nCarrying out the computations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(\\pi ) & =\\frac{1}{2}+{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+{[-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})]}_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+([-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3})]-[-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{3})]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+(-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+(-(-\\frac{1}{2})+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}+1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the position of the particle at $t=\\pi $ is $x=2.$",
